  description="gpubox rents Vast.ai GPU boxes for unattended ML training, probes them on real hardware before use, and destroys them at a deadline. Measured over 791 rentals, including the leak it did not catch."
>
  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Renting GPUs by the hour on a marketplace is cheap and unreliable. Hosts are
    consumer machines with variable disks and networks, offers get taken between
    search and rent, and a box you forget to destroy bills until someone notices.
    gpubox is the set of primitives that let several independent projects share
    one account for multi-hour training jobs without stepping on each other or
    leaking money.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Rents a healthy box, not a listed one.</strong> It races several
      offers at once, probes each on the real hardware (GPU present, VRAM floor,
      disk throughput, CUDA usable), keeps the first winner, and tears down the
      losers. Machines that fail are quarantined with a time limit so the pool
      heals.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Destroys only what it can prove it owns.</strong> Teardown refuses
      unless ownership is recorded, and it releases its record only after the
      provider confirms the box is gone. A box whose destroy call fails stays on
      the retry list instead of being dropped.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A billing ceiling that outlives the laptop.</strong> A detached
      guardian process on an always-on host destroys the box at a wall-clock
      deadline, copying results off first. Until September 20 this backstop had
      never actually fired; see below.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Detached jobs with a liveness signal.</strong> Jobs run under tmux;
      a workload-agnostic watchdog distinguishes a dead job, which is a fact,
      from a stalled one, which is a judgment and only alerts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every offer decision is logged with its propensity.</strong> The
      selector explores 10% of the time, and the full ranked candidate set is
      recorded, so a different selection policy can be scored against the log
      without renting anything.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the data says</h2>
  <p>
    Two months of the event log (July 18 to September 11, 2026): 791 create
    calls, 524 rentals, 360 probe verdicts.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>34% of create calls failed, mostly offers taken between search and rent, and rate limits from firing creates back to back.</li>
    <li>29% of probes failed on SSH, and about half of those machines passed at another time. Real host faults (slow disk, slow network, dead) were 4%.</li>
    <li>Inside the default filter (reliability at least 0.98, disk bandwidth at least 500 MB/s) the host failure rate was 2.3%. Outside it, 71%.</li>
    <li>The cost of every failure over two months was about $4. The cost is time and pool size, not money.</li>
    <li>A learned classifier of good versus bad hosts rediscovered the filter and did worse than the rule, with 15 positive examples. The right next model is a per-machine pass rate shrunk toward a per-GPU prior.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The proof it works: a 1B-parameter training run to step 2000, detached, with
    about 2 GB of checkpoints synced to S3, delivered in 1 hour 57 minutes with
    the box destroyed afterward and zero instances leaked.
  </p>

  <h2>The leak it did not catch</h2>
  <p>
    On September 20 a watcher on an always-on Raspberry Pi kept crashing and
    restarting, and each restart rented a fresh RTX 4090. Nothing destroyed them:
    ten boxes, about $4.40 an hour, until it was found and stopped. The post-mortem
    from the event log found four defects. The worst: three timing constants in
    three different modules disagreed, so every guardian decided it had been
    superseded and exited cleanly without destroying anything. Its exit code
    counted that as success, so the backstop had never once fired and nothing
    looked wrong. A second: the Pi's SSH key was not registered with the account,
    so most probes failed on authentication and innocent hosts were quarantined
    while the race burned through offers. All four are fixed, and a test now pins
    the three constants together.
  </p>

  <h2>What was hard</h2>
  <p>
    Most of the bugs that cost money lived in the seam between the code's assumptions
    and the provider's actual behaviour, and none of them were caught by the 236
    mutation-tested unit tests. They were caught by renting one box for a cent
    and printing the raw output. The method that came out of it: capture real
    output before writing any parser, and mutation-test every load-bearing
    behaviour so a green suite cannot certify a bug.
  </p>
</Project>
